// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  XU FLIGHT STRIP - DATALINK PHRASES  (.cdl)
//
//  What this unit says over the datalink, as one button each. Aurora ignores
//  this file; only the flight strip reads it. Keep it with the sector data so
//  it is reviewed with everything else the division publishes.
//
//  A controller working a touchscreen with one hand should not be typing a
//  clearance at the moment it is needed. Write the sentence once, here, and it
//  goes out with one press - filled in from the strip it is going to.
//
//  Below is exactly what the boards send today. Change the wording your unit
//  says differently, delete what you never use, and commit it: this file then
//  REPLACES the built-in set rather than adding to it.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  COLUMNS   label ; message ; kind ;
//
//    label     what the button says. Short - it is read at a glance.
//    message   the sentence that is sent.
//    kind      which part of the datalink it is, and which strips it is for.
//              Several, in any order, separated by spaces or commas.
//
//  THE THREE SYSTEMS. Datalink is three things with three sets of rules, and a
//  phrase has to say which it is or the board cannot know who may send it:
//
//    TELEX   a plain message. Never a substitute for voice. Anybody may send
//            one, so this is what a phrase is when the column is left empty.
//    CPDLC   an en-route instruction. Only the units the division has given
//            CPDLC to, and only above the level it set. Everyone else: voice.
//    DCL     a departure clearance. Only at the aerodromes the division lists,
//            by whoever is doing the clearance delivery job.
//
//  Who holds each one is entered on Settings, Division - not here. This file
//  says what the words are; the division says who may say them.
//
//  AND WHICH STRIPS. DEP or ARR narrows a phrase to one side of the flight. A
//  start up approval marked DEP is never offered on an aircraft on final.
//
//  There is NO airport column and no station names. What a unit says is the
//  same sentence everywhere, and the things that change are answered already -
//  the runway and the wind by the ATIS, who the aircraft goes to next by the
//  .hof handoff file. Writing "CONTACT SCOTTISH CONTROL 129.225" here would be
//  the handoff order copied into a second file, to go stale the first time a
//  sector is rearranged.
//
//  Every line ends with a semicolon, the way Aurora's own sector files end
//  theirs: it is what says the sentence is finished. One line is one message -
//  there is no continuation character. Line the columns up as neatly as you
//  like; runs of spaces are collapsed, so the wire gets one.
//
//  EVERY MESSAGE GOES OUT WITH THE CALLSIGN ON THE FRONT. The wire already
//  carries the address, but what a pilot reads is the text - a bare "START UP
//  APPROVED" on a flight deck with three messages open is one they have to
//  match up themselves. So write the sentence, not the callsign - NO line
//  below writes {CALLSIGN} out, not even the clearance. Write it anyway and it
//  is left alone rather than said twice, but the token is for the middle of a
//  sentence, not the front of one.
//
//    START UP  ; START UP APPROVED ; TELEX DEP ;  ->  EZY88DK START UP APPROVED
//
//  TOKENS    filled from the strip, and dropped when the board does not know
//            them - along with the word in front, so a phrase never goes out
//            with braces on the wire. Not case-sensitive, and a plural is the
//            same token: {WIND}, {wind} and {winds} all put the wind in.
//
//    {CALLSIGN}           the callsign          {TYPE}    aircraft type
//    {DEP_AIRPORT}        where it came from
//    {ARR_AIRPORT}        where it is going
//    {AIRPORT}            the aerodrome you are working
//    {RUNWAY}             the runway in use     {SID}     its SID
//    {STAR}               its arrival           {FIX}     next fix on the strip
//    {SQUAWK}             the code assigned     {LEVEL}   the level on the strip
//    {HEADING}  {SPEED}   as written on the strip
//    {HANDOFF_CALLSIGN}   who it goes to next, from the .hof order
//    {HANDOFF_FREQ}       that station's frequency, from who is online
//    {WIND}  {QNH}  {ATIS}  {TA}  {TL}   off this board's own status bar
//
//  NOTES     anything after // or # is ignored. Write why a phrase exists
//            beside it: the next person to open this file is the one who needs
//            that sentence.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ── DCL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Listed aerodromes only, by whoever is doing the clearance delivery job.
PDC         ; PDC - CLEARED TO {ARR_AIRPORT} VIA {SID} DEPARTURE SQUAWK {SQUAWK} ; DCL ;

// ── AIRBORNE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// En-route instructions. The division's CPDLC units only, above the level it
// set; everyone below that works these by voice.
CLIMB       ; CLIMB TO {LEVEL}                                  ; CPDLC ;
DESCEND     ; DESCEND TO {LEVEL}                                ; CPDLC ;
MAINTAIN    ; MAINTAIN {LEVEL}                                  ; CPDLC ;
REP LEVEL   ; REPORT MAINTAINING {LEVEL}                        ; CPDLC ;
HEADING     ; FLY HEADING {HEADING} DEGREES                     ; CPDLC ;
SPEED       ; SPEED {SPEED} KNOTS                               ; CPDLC ;
DIRECT      ; PROCCED DIRECT TO {FIX}                           ; CPDLC ;
SQUAWK      ; SQUAWK {SQUAWK}                                   ; CPDLC ;
QNH         ; QNH {QNH}                                         ; CPDLC ;
TL          ; TRANSITION LEVEL {TL}                             ; CPDLC ;

// ── COMMS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Plain messages. Anybody may send one, and none of them replaces a call.
CONTACT     ; CONTACT {HANDOFF_CALLSIGN} {HANDOFF_FREQ}         ; TELEX ;
MONITOR     ; MONITOR {HANDOFF_CALLSIGN} {HANDOFF_FREQ}         ; TELEX ;
UNICOM      ; MONITOR UNICOM 122.800                            ; TELEX ;
ATIS        ; ATIS INFORMATION {ATIS}                           ; TELEX ;
STANDBY     ; STANDBY                                           ; TELEX ;
ROGER       ; ROGER                                             ; TELEX ;
UNABLE      ; UNABLE                                            ; TELEX ;
AFFIRM      ; AFFIRM                                            ; TELEX ;
NEGATIVE    ; NEGATIVE                                          ; TELEX ;
SAY AGAIN   ; SAY AGAIN                                         ; TELEX ;
